{"tool_name":"deca-hdx","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"deca-hdx","home":"http://github.com/komiveslab/DECA","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Comprehensive, Automatic Post-processing Program for HDX-MS Data.","addition_date":"2020-01-09T17:44:26Z","last_update_date":"2020-12-17T14:38:26Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:deca-hdx","credit":[{"email":"mlinderman@middlebury.edu","name":"Michael D. Linderman","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9643-7148","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"ekomives@ucsd.edu","name":"Elizabeth A. Komives","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Deisotoping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3629"},{"term":"Essential dynamics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3891"},{"term":"Molecular dynamics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2476"},{"term":"Variant calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3227"}]}],"name":"Deuterium Exchange Correction and Analysis (DECA)","owner":"Kigaard","publication":[{"doi":"10.1074/MCP.TIR119.001731","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2019 Lumpkin and Komives.Amide hydrogen-deuterium exchange mass spectrometry (HDX-MS) has become widely popular for mapping protein- ligand interfaces, for understanding protein-protein interactions, and for discovering dynamic allostery. Several platforms are now available which provide large data sets of amide hydrogen/deuterium exchange mass spectrometry (HDX-MS) data. Although many of these platforms provide some down-stream processing, a comprehensive software that provides the most commonly used down-stream processing tools such as automatic backexchange correction options, analysis of overlapping peptides, calculations of relative deuterium uptake into regions of the protein after such corrections, rigorous statistical analysis of the significance of uptake differences, and generation of high quality figures for data presentation is not yet available. Here we describe the Deuterium Exchange Correction and Analysis (DECA) software package, which provides all these downstream processing options for data from the most popular mass spectrometry platforms. The major functions of the software are demonstrated on sample data.","authors":[{"name":"Komives E.A."},{"name":"Lumpkin R.J."}],"citationCount":11,"date":"2019-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Molecular and Cellular Proteomics","title":"DECA, a comprehensive, automatic post-processing program for HDX-MS data"},"pmcid":"PMC6885705","pmid":"31594786"}],"topic":[{"term":"Biophysics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3306"},{"term":"Proteomics experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3520"},{"term":"Small molecules","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0154"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Deuterium Exchange Correction and Analysis (DECA)","home":"https://bio.tools/deca-hdx","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Comprehensive, Automatic Post-processing Program for HDX-MS Data.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:deca-hdx/app/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":true,"registration":false},"packages":true,"source_packages":true,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":true,"license":true,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":33,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":2,"year":"2025"},{"count":4,"year":"2021"},{"count":6,"year":"2020"},{"count":6,"year":"2022"},{"count":6,"year":"2023"},{"count":8,"year":"2024"}],"doi":"10.1074/mcp.TIR119.001731","pmcid":"PMC6885705","pmid":"31594786","ref_count":28,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1998"},{"count":1,"year":"1999"},{"cou